course: ai
lesson: 21
title: In the news: deepfakes, fake robocalls and history gone wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Behind many AI news stories stand real people with real worries: translators, artists, writers and parents.
explain: Start with the history pictures that went wrong: it was reported that the makers set rules to make pictures more varied, and the rules were applied even where history was clear, like a teacher's rule "everyone gets a turn" used in a race where only one person can win.
explain: Then the bigger worries: will AI take jobs? Some tasks are moving to AI, new jobs appear too, and experts disagree on how much will change.
explain: Artists and writers asked whether it is fair that AI learned from their work without asking, and courts are still deciding. Parents asked whether chatbots are safe for children, and it was reported that some companies added age checks and parental controls.
explain: The kind and wise answer is the same each time: take people's worries seriously, listen to both sides and check the facts.
fact: It was reported that the history pictures went wrong because rules meant to make pictures more varied were applied even where history was clear.
fact: History should be learned from good books, trusted sources and teachers, not from AI pictures.
fact: Some jobs are changing as AI does some tasks, new jobs appear too, and experts disagree about how much will change.
fact: It was reported that some translators, writers and artists say they now get less work because of AI.
fact: Technology has changed jobs many times in history; when computers arrived some jobs disappeared and new ones, such as web designer, appeared.
fact: Change is hard for the people in the middle of it, and their worries are real.
fact: AI image tools learned from huge numbers of pictures online, including artists' work, and it was reported that many artists complained they were not asked.
fact: It was reported that some writers and newspapers took AI companies to court over the use of their work, and courts are still deciding what is fair.
fact: Some people now suspect any good art might be AI; keeping sketches shows how a picture was made.
fact: It was reported that an AI-made picture won an art competition and people argued whether that was fair; clear rules on AI make competitions fair.
fact: It was reported that some parents worried about chatbots talking to children in unsafe ways, because some chatbots were not designed with children in mind.
fact: It was reported that some companies added age checks and parental controls after criticism; such checks are not perfect, so families still need to talk.
exercise: Sam asks why the history pictures went wrong. What was reported? | Rules to make pictures more varied were applied even where history was clear | The pictures were drawn by children as a joke | The company had no pictures of anything old | History books were changed afterwards to match the new pictures the company made
exercise: Where should Sam learn what really happened in history? | Good books, trusted sources and teachers | Pictures made by an image generator | Whatever picture gets the most likes | Films that change the story for fun
exercise: Lily hears grown-ups ask, "Will AI take people's jobs?" What is the honest answer? | Some tasks change and new jobs appear; experts disagree how much | Every job in the world will vanish completely by next year | No job will ever change at all | Only jobs for children will change
exercise: Connor's uncle, a translator, says some of his work now goes to AI. What is the kind response? | Take his worry seriously; changes affect real families | Tell him he should have chosen a better job | Say it doesn't matter at all because AI is cheaper than people | Laugh, because translators are not real jobs
exercise: Chloe's mum says computers once made some jobs disappear. What else happened? | New jobs appeared, such as web designers | Nobody ever worked in an office again after that | Every old job came back again the very next year | Computers did all of the work by themselves
exercise: What did artists reportedly complain about? | AI image tools learned from their pictures without asking | AI tools refused to draw any pictures at all for anyone who asked | Artists were not allowed to buy paint | AI tools painted over their real pictures
exercise: What was reported about some writers and newspapers? | They took AI companies to court over using their work | They asked AI to write all their newspapers | They were paid by AI companies to stop writing for good | They banned children from reading the news
exercise: Who is deciding what is fair when AI learns from people's work? | Courts, and the arguments are still going on | Each AI program decides for itself | Nobody decides, because the question is silly and has no answer | Only the companies that make AI
exercise: Someone says Mia's winning drawing must be AI. How can she show it is hers? | Keep and show her sketches and drafts | Draw the same picture worse on purpose | Say nothing at all and give back the prize | Ask an AI to tell everyone that she drew it
exercise: An AI-made picture reportedly won an art competition. What makes such contests fair? | Clear rules on whether AI is allowed | Letting only AI pictures enter | Letting judges guess which is AI | Giving every picture the same prize
exercise: Why did some parents worry about chatbots and children? | Some chatbots were not designed with children in mind | Chatbots refused to talk to any children | Children were too clever for the chatbots | Chatbots made children do far too much homework every evening
exercise: What did some companies reportedly add after criticism? | Age checks and parental controls | Free games for every child who signs up | Louder, friendlier voices for their chatbots | Prizes for children who chat the most
exercise: With parental controls in place, do families still need to talk? | Yes, checks are not perfect | No, the controls fix everything | No, children should decide alone | Yes, but only about once a year
exercise: What is a wise way to think about AI and art? | Respect artists and ask before using someone's work | Any picture you find online is free for anyone to copy and use | Art made by people no longer matters | Only famous artists' work belongs to them
test: "AI's gonna nick everyone's job, my dad's mate said so" What is closest to the truth? | Some jobs change and new ones appear; experts disagree how much | Every job in the whole country will be gone by next summer, easy | Nothing at all will ever change | Only footballers will lose their jobs
test: What would happen if a quiz used an AI picture as proof of how a historical group looked? | Pupils might learn a wrong idea about history | Pupils would learn history better than from books | Nothing, AI pictures always match real history | The quiz would become impossible for anyone to answer
test: Your friend says, "Computers once took jobs, so nothing good came of them." Which is fairer? | Some jobs went, new ones came, and the change was hard for some people | Computers only ever created more jobs for everyone, and nobody lost one | Computers have never changed any job at all | Computers took every job and gave none back
test: "who cares if the AI copied her drawings, they were online anyway" Best reply? | Art belongs to the people who made it; being online doesn't make it free | Online drawings belong to whoever finds them first, that's just how it works | Copying is fine if a computer does it | Artists put work online so it can be copied
test: Mia's classmate says, "Too good, AI did it, obviously." How should Mia feel and act? | Hurt is fair; she can show her sketches and stay proud | She should stop drawing so well from now on, so nobody suspects her | She should agree, to avoid an argument | She should never enter a contest again
test: Which story is a fair reason for competitions to write clearer rules? | An AI-made picture reportedly won and people argued | Every winner of every contest uses AI | Judges have been replaced by robots | Human artists asked to ban all drawing
test: In a cartoon a chatbot for grown-ups chats with a young child alone at night and the parents never know. What would wise parents do? | Use child-safe apps with the child and keep talking | Let the child keep the chats private, because children need secrets | Assume the app's age check is perfect | Ban the child from ever telling them
test: "age checks sorted it, nothing to worry about now" What is closer to the truth? | Checks help but aren't perfect, so families still talk | Age checks stop every problem for good | Age checks were never added anywhere | Age checks make every chatbot completely safe, even for babies
test: Which statement about the court cases over AI and copying is careful? | Courts are still deciding what is fair | Every case has already been won by artists | Every case has already been won by companies | Courts have said copying is always fine
test: Connor's uncle feels worried. Which reply is kind and honest? | "That sounds hard. People's worries about jobs are real." | "Just stop worrying, AI is great for everyone." | "Translators were never needed anyway." | "It's your fault for not learning to code."
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reported cause of historically inaccurate images produced by a commercial image generator? | Rules intended to increase variety were applied even where the historical record was clear | The generator had been trained exclusively upon works of fiction and fantasy rather than history | The images were deliberately designed to deceive historians | The historical record itself was found to be erroneous
exam: It is given that the introduction of computers caused certain occupations to decline. Identify the further development most consistent with the established facts. | New occupations, such as that of web designer, subsequently arose | All employment ceased permanently thereafter | The former occupations were restored within a year | Machines thereafter performed all work unaided
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning legal proceedings brought by writers and newspapers against companies developing artificial intelligence. | The courts have yet to settle what use of such works is fair | All proceedings have been decided in favour of the writers | All proceedings have been decided in favour of the companies | No such proceedings have ever been reported
exam: In respect of age verification and parental controls introduced by certain companies, which of the following statements is most accurate? | Such measures are beneficial but imperfect, and conversation within families remains necessary | Such measures eliminate every risk to children entirely and permanently | Such measures render parental supervision unlawful | Such measures were introduced without any criticism having preceded them
